//! The registration of the tool `import_html` (module M1 of `docs/standards/modular-thin-ui.md`): its name, the text an agent reads and its risk tier.
//!
//! The importer itself is [`crate::map::import`]; the adapter that serves the tool over MCP is
//! the module `html_import` of `meldframe-http`.

use meldframe_module::{Module, Registrar};

/// The name of the agent tool.
pub const TOOL: &str = "import_html";

/// The tool text an agent reads (English, ASCII, part of the contract; `docs/mcp-tools.json` lists it).
pub const DESCRIPTION: &str = "Import HTML and CSS text that you read from the user's project as nodes of a file, so you can pull a section of a page onto the canvas, bind variables to it, edit it and export it again. The input is text: html (a fragment or a whole document) and optionally css (more rules, read after every style block, so at equal specificity it wins); html and css together are at most 262144 bytes (256 KiB, counted in bytes). Nothing is fetched, run or followed: a link, an @import, a url() and an image src are never opened, a script is never run and no browser is used. The importer reads the CSS the author wrote, it does not compute the page the way a browser does, so inline the linked style sheets into css yourself, and if you have a browser, send HTML whose styles are inline and already in px. Read are: style attributes, style blocks and css (type, class, id, attribute, universal, descendant and child selectors, lists, !important, var(), the shorthands of margin, padding, border, border-radius, gap, flex, overflow, font and background), the classes that export_frame writes in its tailwind format (send the css it returned together with that html: the reset and the variables live there), and a small table of browser defaults (headings, paragraph and list margins, the 8 px body margin). Tailwind's own scale and palette (p-4, bg-blue-500) and variants (hover:, md:) are not read: the class is listed, send the compiled CSS in css. Mapping: a flex container is a row or column frame, a block is a column frame with no gap, a text is a text node (a different inline style inside it joins the text and each difference is listed), an element with a background, border, shadow or padding that holds text is a frame with a text in it, an image or svg is a rect of its size with fill none (the src is listed under assets, nothing is fetched: upload the file with upload_asset and set the image with apply_commands), a button, input, textarea and select are frames with their label, a table is a column of rows whose cells share the row, display none and hidden elements are skipped, a wrapper that says nothing is not a node. Sizes are fixed (px), hug (fit-content, nowrap text, a flex item) or fill (a block or stretched child, flex 1, width 100%); the model size is the border box, so a content-box element grows by its padding and border. Whatever the model cannot hold is listed on the node it affects under unsupported with a stable code, never guessed: line-height, font-family other than Inter, min and max sizes, transforms, grid, wrap, per-side borders, inner shadows, filters, @media. A node row lists at most 32 lines under each of approximated and unsupported, and omitted counts the lines left out of the two (strict counts every property, listed or not). A value changed to the nearest the model holds is listed under approximated (rem to px, a font weight to a step of 100, 50 percent radius with children). Colours become hex, and var() is replaced by its value: nothing is bound to a variable. The text of a node is kept as written; names come from data-name, id, the first class or the role of the element. The whole import is one atomic batch of at most 100 commands (one node each): a page that makes more is refused as E_IMPORT_HTML_TOO_MANY, so send one section at a time, each under the parentId of the frame it belongs in. Without parentId the root is a new top-level frame to the right of the others (the page is 1440 px wide unless the CSS or width says another); name is the name of the root, else the title, else Imported HTML. The call adds nodes and never changes or removes one, so it takes no ifSeq (an add cannot conflict): a parent that was removed meanwhile is E_PARENT_NOT_FOUND and one that is not a frame E_PARENT_NOT_FRAME. strict true refuses the call as E_IMPORT_HTML_UNSUPPORTED when any property was not carried, listing them, and writes nothing; dryRun true writes nothing and returns the same result, with the refusals the file would give. Every call needs an idempotencyKey of 1 to 87 characters (also accepted as idempotency_key), except a dryRun; send the same call again after a timeout and nothing is written twice (replayed is true), while the same key with other text is a new call, and two actors that send the same key and text each make their own nodes. A dryRun with a key makes the ids of the call that will write; without a key it makes them from the key dry-run, so they differ. note is as in apply_commands (at most 500 characters and 3 line breaks) and the import is one numbered batch in the Activity panel, so a person can revert it there: its texts and shapes are reverted, and the frames it made stay, empty (nested frames too), because removing a frame needs a person's approval; you cannot undo it yourself, for the same reason. The result lists root, commands, one row for each node (id, name, type, source element such as section.hero#2, approximated, unsupported), skipped elements, assets, warnings (default layer names, layout findings, text a browser breaks differently, text sizes the server only estimates: text boxes are measured by the server's stub, not the real font; when any text is over 4096 characters no layout finding is worked out) and the report of the readers (counts, and at most 200 entries). Errors: E_IMPORT_HTML_TOO_LARGE (over 256 KiB), E_IMPORT_HTML_TOO_MANY_ELEMENTS (over 5000 elements), E_IMPORT_HTML_EMPTY (nothing makes a node), E_IMPORT_HTML_TOO_MANY, E_IMPORT_HTML_UNSUPPORTED, and the codes of apply_commands with the index of the command and the source element it came from.";

/// The HTML import module. It registers one Change-tier agent tool: an import adds nodes to the file.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct ImportHtmlModule;

impl Module for ImportHtmlModule {
    fn name(&self) -> &'static str {
        "html-import"
    }

    fn register(&self, registrar: &mut Registrar) {
        registrar.change_tool(TOOL, DESCRIPTION);
    }
}
